<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!-- Pairing, both ends of it: handing the key to a new device over the LAN, authenticated by a code
     read off one screen and typed into the other.

     One layout for two roles, because they are the same conversation seen from opposite sides and
     share every part of it: a title, a line of explanation, something turning while waiting, and one
     button. The provider shows a code and waits; the joiner looks, picks, and types the code it is
     being shown. PairSheet turns the pieces on and off.

     The same surface as the peer sheet — a bottom sheet, which also means predictive back for free.
     24dp gutters, matching it, so the two read as one app. -->
<LinearLayout xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    xmlns:app="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res-auto"
    android:id="@+id/pair_root"
    android:layout_width="match_parent"
    android:layout_height="wrap_content"
    android:orientation="vertical"
    android:paddingBottom="16dp">

    <com.google.android.material.bottomsheet.BottomSheetDragHandleView
        android:layout_width="match_parent"
        android:layout_height="wrap_content" />

    <TextView
        android:id="@+id/pair_title"
        android:layout_width="match_parent"
        android:layout_height="wrap_content"
        android:paddingHorizontal="24dp"
        android:textAppearance="?attr/textAppearanceTitleLarge"
        android:textColor="?attr/colorOnSurface" />

    <!-- The one line of explanation, rewritten at every state. minLines, and this is the reason it
         is here rather than left to wrap_content: this view carries eight different strings over the
         life of the sheet — the two bodies, "Found these…", "Enter the code shown on…", and four
         endings — and they are not the same number of lines. Left to itself the sheet grew and shrank
         by a line of body text every time one replaced another, which on the joining side is three
         times in the few seconds between picking a device and being paired. Two lines is what the
         longest of the ordinary ones takes on a narrow phone, so reserving two costs nothing in the
         states that need two and holds the sheet still in the ones that need one.

         minLines and not a fixed height: it is a reservation, not a cap. Anything longer still wraps,
         and the blank line a shorter string leaves behind is part of the same TextView, so TalkBack
         reads the string and nothing else — there is no empty view here to focus. -->
    <TextView
        android:id="@+id/pair_text"
        android:layout_width="match_parent"
        android:layout_height="wrap_content"
        android:paddingHorizontal="24dp"
        android:paddingTop="4dp"
        android:lineSpacingExtra="2dp"
        android:minLines="2"
        android:textAppearance="?attr/textAppearanceBodyMedium"
        android:textColor="?attr/colorOnSurfaceVariant" />

    <!-- The code, on the device that holds the key. Display type and tracking because it is read
         aloud across a room and typed on a phone in the other hand — eight digits that run together
         are eight digits typed wrong. Monospace so every digit takes the same width and the eye can
         group them without the text reflowing as they change.

         0.25, up from the 0.15 nine digits needed and still below the 0.3 this carried at six.
         Monospace tracking adds to every glyph including the last, so the line's advance is
         n × (1 + spacing) em: nine digits at 0.15 was 10.35 em and that was the width that just fit
         a narrow phone inside the 24dp gutters. Eight at 0.25 is 10.0 em — under the width already
         proven to fit — while 0.3 would be 10.4 em, i.e. very slightly over it. So 0.25 is the last
         value that buys separation without going back to guessing. The gutters are not negotiable.

         This is a single-line TextView, so tracking changes its width and never its height: the
         three-state equal-height layout the rest of this sheet is built around (see pair_progress
         and PairSheet.KEEP) is untouched by the number above.

         It starts as eight greyed digits rather than empty, and that is what stops the sheet jumping
         open. The code cannot exist yet when this is laid out — deriving it is a deliberately slow
         scrypt on a worker — so an empty view meant the sheet measured itself without a line of display
         type and then grew by exactly that much a moment later. Digits and not dashes because the
         two do not draw to the same height: a row of dashes reserved the width but not the line. -->
    <TextView
        android:id="@+id/pair_code"
        android:layout_width="match_parent"
        android:layout_height="wrap_content"
        android:layout_marginTop="16dp"
        android:paddingHorizontal="24dp"
        android:gravity="center"
        android:fontFamily="monospace"
        android:letterSpacing="0.25"
        android:text="@string/pair_code_placeholder"
        android:textAppearance="?attr/textAppearanceDisplaySmall"
        android:textColor="?attr/colorPrimary"
        android:visibility="gone" />

    <!-- Waiting. Indeterminate is honest about a browse: nothing here knows how long a multicast
         answer takes, and a determinate bar would be inventing one.

         LoadingIndicator, which is M3 Expressive's own — a morphing sequence of shapes rather than
         the spinning arc of CircularProgressIndicator. The arc is the Material 2 shape that M3
         inherited, and next to Expressive's springy motion everywhere else on this surface it reads
         as a different app's idea of waiting. Left on its default style deliberately: its size and
         colours come from the theme, which is the whole point of using the component instead of
         drawing one.

         On the joining side this row is INVISIBLE rather than GONE for part of its life, and that is
         deliberate — see PairSheet.KEEP. Typing a code, waiting for it to be checked, and being told
         it was wrong are three states of one screen, and this row and the button below take turns:
         the row appears exactly when the button goes. Removing either one's space while the other
         arrives moved the sheet's top edge twice in a second, once in each direction. Holding this
         row's space through all three is what makes that screen one height. Do not "save a
         measurement" by turning it back into GONE; the measurement is what is being paid for. -->
    <LinearLayout
        android:id="@+id/pair_progress"
        android:layout_width="match_parent"
        android:layout_height="wrap_content"
        android:layout_marginTop="16dp"
        android:paddingHorizontal="24dp"
        android:gravity="center_vertical"
        android:orientation="horizontal"
        android:visibility="gone">

        <com.google.android.material.loadingindicator.LoadingIndicator
            android:layout_width="wrap_content"
            android:layout_height="wrap_content" />

        <TextView
            android:id="@+id/pair_progress_text"
            android:layout_width="match_parent"
            android:layout_height="wrap_content"
            android:layout_marginStart="16dp"
            android:textAppearance="?attr/textAppearanceBodyMedium"
            android:textColor="?attr/colorOnSurfaceVariant" />
    </LinearLayout>

    <!-- Devices found, one card each, filled in by PairSheet. -->
    <LinearLayout
        android:id="@+id/pair_list"
        android:layout_width="match_parent"
        android:layout_height="wrap_content"
        android:layout_marginTop="12dp"
        android:orientation="vertical"
        android:visibility="gone" />

    <!-- errorEnabled up front, which is the opposite of what Ui.showError does elsewhere in this app
         and is the point: there the error line is collapsed when the complaint goes, because a
         settings form has a dozen fields and a dozen permanent gaps. Here there is one field, and
         everything it can be told — "Eight digits", or what the connection failed with — arrives
         while the user is staring at the sheet waiting for a verdict. Enabling the line from the
         start reserves it once, so a wrong code adds a message instead of adding a message AND a row
         of height. The reserved line is empty text in a view with no content, so it is not announced
         and cannot be focused.

         PairSheet therefore calls setError directly and never Ui.showError: that helper's
         setErrorEnabled(false) would undo this on the first cleared error. -->
    <com.google.android.material.textfield.TextInputLayout
        android:id="@+id/pair_code_layout"
        style="?attr/textInputOutlinedStyle"
        android:layout_width="match_parent"
        android:layout_height="wrap_content"
        android:layout_marginTop="16dp"
        android:layout_marginHorizontal="24dp"
        android:hint="@string/pair_code_hint"
        app:errorEnabled="true"
        android:visibility="gone">

        <!-- number, not numberPassword or text: the code is not a secret from the person holding the
             phone, and a digits-only keyboard is the whole point. maxLength must track
             Pairing.CODE_DIGITS — PairSheet refuses anything shorter, and a field that accepted nine
             digits would let the user type one the provider can never match.

             (This comment used to sit between the attributes of the tag below, where XML does not
             allow one.) -->
        <com.google.android.material.textfield.TextInputEditText
            android:id="@+id/pair_code_input"
            android:layout_width="match_parent"
            android:layout_height="wrap_content"
            android:inputType="number"
            android:maxLength="8"
            android:imeOptions="actionDone"
            android:fontFamily="monospace"
            android:letterSpacing="0.2" />
    </com.google.android.material.textfield.TextInputLayout>

    <!-- The one button. It is the other half of the exchange described on pair_progress: while a
         code is being checked this goes INVISIBLE and not GONE, so the row above it can appear into
         a sheet that does not move. INVISIBLE also takes it out of the accessibility tree and out of
         reach of a tap, which is what is wanted anyway — there is nothing to press while waiting. -->
    <com.google.android.material.button.MaterialButton
        android:id="@+id/pair_action"
        android:layout_width="wrap_content"
        android:layout_height="wrap_content"
        android:layout_gravity="end"
        android:layout_marginTop="16dp"
        android:layout_marginHorizontal="24dp"
        android:visibility="gone" />
</LinearLayout>
